  "doc": [
    "*Chrysolina coerulans*, dorsal — the blue mint beetle.",
    "",
    "The fourth Hochdanz lithograph from Calwer's *Käferbuch* (1876), and the",
    "smallest reference in the folder at 229 × 250 pixels — smaller even than the",
    "seven-spot's. That turned out not to matter for the same reason it did not",
    "matter there: what a leaf beetle needs from a reference is the outline, the",
    "puncture rows and where the short legs sit, and all three survive at that",
    "size. `references/chrysolina-coerulans.jpg` is the file.",
    "",
    "## What the drawing has to get right",
    "",
    "The **outline**, which is the whole animal. A Chrysolina is 0.65 as wide as",
    "it is long and strongly domed — very nearly a hemisphere — and the tracing",
    "reflex is to draw the beetle-shaped oval that every other Coleoptera plate",
    "here wanted. Beside the violet ground beetle at 0.39 the difference is the",
    "identification, so the margin is measured row by row off the lithograph.",
    "",
    "Then the **legs**, which are short, thick and held close in. A leaf beetle",
    "spends its life on one plant and walks nowhere; drawn at the ground beetle's",
    "proportions it becomes a different family.",
    "",
    "The blue is not drawn, because it cannot be: the animal is metallic and the",
    "plate is inked in one seasonal earth. `morphology.colourFamily` says",
    "`metallic` in words instead, which is where that fact belongs.",
    "",
    "## Plate space",
    "",
    "y = 0 at the front of the head, y = 1000 at the elytral apex. The head is",
    "mostly under the pronotum — 90 to 310 — which is why the visible head is",
    "only the first 150 units. The antennae reach above y = -160 and the legs out",
    "to about x = 480."
  ],
  "parts": [
    {
      "note": [
        "The legs first, so they pass under the body.",
        "",
        "Short and stout: the whole leg from coxa to claw is about a body length",
        "in the lithograph, against nearly two for the ground beetle. Femora at",
        "around seven per cent of the width across the wing cases — a smaller",
        "fraction than the Carabus, because this beetle is so much broader."
      ],
